this morning 11 members of a far-right  extremist group the oath keepers are  charged with conspiracy to overthrow the  u.s government the justice department  accuses the suspects of creating a  battle plan for last year's assault on  the capital these are by far the most  serious charges today in connection with  the january 6 riot senior investigative  correspondent katherine herridge has the  story  more than a thousand miles from  washington d.c federal agents arrested  stuart rose the leader and founder of  the oath keepers at his home in texas  the newly unsealed indictment marks the  first time rhodes a former army  paratrooper and edward vallejo of  arizona are charged in connection with  the riots rhodes and 10 co-defendants  are accused of a months-long conspiracy  to oppose by force the transfer of  presidential power thomas o'connor was  an fbi agent with the joint terrorism  task force for almost 23 years we never  brought a charge of sedition it is  extremely serious it is the highest  level  on january 6 prosecutors allege the oath  keepers marched in two stack formations  to breach the building  once inside it's alleged one team headed  toward the senate chamber and a second  team went in search of house speaker  pelosi  outside the capitol prosecutors say the  oath keepers had a quick reaction force  prepared to rapidly transport firearms  and other weapons  as the violence unfolded rhodes  allegedly expressed his frustration with  the trump administration via encrypted  messaging pence is doing nothing as i  predicted all i see trump doing is  complaining i see no intent by him to do  anything so the patriots are taking it  into their own hands they've had enough  roses due in a texas court later today a  conviction could bring up to 20 years in  prison contacted by cbs news one of his  lawyers questioned whether the  indictment contains new significant  evidence against his client  gail  all right catherine thank you very much
 

